CAVEAT:  The FLOOD half of "flood and prune" is NOT MEASURED. Three never-joined groups
         were streamed (40, 50 and 25 frames at 4-5 frames/s) with a tcpdump armed on
         vlan 61 before the stream in two of the three, and not one data frame of any of
         them appeared on the transit segment or at the receiver. The PRUNE is
         unambiguous (STEP 3's empty outgoing interface list, STEP 4's Join/Prune, and
         the fact that a prune cannot exist without something to prune), so the
         mechanism is not in doubt; what is not demonstrated is the brief INITIAL flood.
         On this build State Refresh is active (STEP 4), which is precisely the feature
         that suppresses PIM-DM's periodic re-flood, and the first-packet flood
         converges to a pruned outgoing list faster than a 0.2 s inter-frame gap can
         sample. To measure it, a future run needs a line-rate stimulus
         (tools/linerate.py or tcpreplay) with a capture on vlan 61 and per-frame
         timestamps, so the flood/prune boundary can be located in time rather than
         counted. This is a limitation of the stimulus, not a platform fault, and it is
         declared rather than claimed. Recorded as O-25.
         Separately, PIM-DM ASSERT is not reachable on this bench: it needs two PIM
         routers on ONE shared segment and the only multi-router segment here is a /30.
         swi_a was configured but never reloaded or power-cycled, so reboot persistence
         is proven for the DUT only.

