STEP 4 -- the PIM-DM control plane decoded on the transit wire (designed)          PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus#tcpdump -i vlan61 -c 24 -nn             (during a stream to an unjoined group)
04:05:50.847472 IP 10.0.61.1 > 224.0.0.13: PIMv2, Prune Refresh, length 36
04:05:58.028256 IP 10.0.61.2 > 224.0.0.13: PIMv2, Join / Prune, length 34
... and repeatedly thereafter:
04:07:08.159471 IP 10.0.61.1 > 224.0.0.13: PIMv2, Prune Refresh, length 36
04:07:11.587323 IP 10.0.61.1 > 224.0.0.13: PIMv2, Prune Refresh, length 36
04:07:13.747242 IP 10.0.61.2 > 224.0.0.13: PIMv2, Join / Prune, length 34
04:07:50.851472 IP 10.0.61.1 > 224.0.0.13: PIMv2, Prune Refresh, length 36

  => STEP 4 PASS: `sw1` (10.0.61.2) sends Join/Prune upstream to prune the branch, and
     the DUT (10.0.61.1) sends Prune Refresh -- PIM-DM State Refresh, RFC 3973 section
     4.5 -- periodically to keep that prune alive. Both messages are PIM-DM-specific;
     neither appears in the PIM-SM captures taken on this same wire, which carried
     Register and Register-Stop instead. This is the cleanest protocol-level proof that
     what is running is dense mode and not sparse mode.

